AI/MCP Overview
AI

!!!Introduction

The <*AI/MCP*> library (<*PocoAIMCP*>, namespace <[Poco::AI::MCP]>) implements the
Model Context Protocol (MCP), revision 2026-07-28: the server side, the client side over
any transport, and the stdio transport. The <*AI/MCP/HTTP*> library (<*PocoAIMCPHTTP*>,
namespace <[Poco::AI::MCP::HTTP]>) adds the Streamable HTTP client transport.

Dependencies: AI/MCP needs Foundation and JSON; AI/MCP/HTTP needs Net as well, and NetSSL for
https endpoints.


!!!The Protocol

MCP connects an application that uses a language model, the client, with programs that offer
it tools, resources and prompts, the servers. Messages are JSON-RPC 2.0.

Revision 2026-07-28 is stateless. There is no initialization handshake and no session: every
request names the protocol revision, the client's capabilities and the client's info in its
<*_meta*> object, and every result says it is complete and carries the server's info. A
client sends <*server/discover*> first, to learn whether the server implements the revision
and what it offers. These libraries implement no earlier revision; a server of an earlier
revision answers <*server/discover*> with an error.


!!!Servers

<[Server]> holds the tools, resources and prompts an application registers, and answers
requests through any transport: one message at a time with <[Server::handle()]>, or as
newline-delimited JSON on a pair of streams with <[Server::serve()]>, which is the stdio
transport when the streams are <*std::cin*> and <*std::cout*>.

A tool is described by a <[Tool]>: its name, description, input schema, optional output
schema and annotations, and the handler that runs it. A handler gets the arguments and a
<[RequestContext]>, with the identity a transport established and the protocol revision and
client of the request, and returns a <[ToolResult]>: content blocks (text, images, audio,
resource links, embedded resources), optional structured content, and an error flag for a
tool that failed. Resources are read by a reader that returns <[ResourceContents]>, prompts
are rendered by a renderer that returns their messages.

Registration is thread-safe, and handlers run on the thread that calls <[Server::handle()]>
or <[Server::serve()]>. One server serves any number of clients at once.


!!!Clients

<[Client]> is the client side of the protocol, written once over the exchange of a
transport: <[Client::discover()]>, <[Client::listTools()]>, <[Client::callTool()]>,
<[Client::listResources()]>, <[Client::readResource()]>, <[Client::listPrompts()]>,
<[Client::getPrompt()]>, and <[Client::call()]> for other methods. Lists are fetched page by
page. An error reply becomes an <[MCPException]> with the JSON-RPC error code in
<[Poco::Exception::code()]>.

The transports:

  - <[StreamClient]> exchanges newline-delimited JSON over a pair of streams.
  - <[ProcessClient]> launches a server program and talks to it over its standard input and
    output: the stdio transport. The server gets the environment of the application, or only
    the variables it is given. Closing it ends the server: it closes the server's standard
    input, asks the server's process group to end, and kills it last.
  - <[Poco::AI::MCP::HTTP::HTTPClient]> speaks the Streamable HTTP transport over one
    keep-alive connection, with a byte limit on replies and a deadline for every request. A
    request hook adds headers such as <*Authorization*>.

Whatever a server returns is untrusted input: tool descriptions, annotations and results may
have been written by an attacker, and a model that reads them may be led to call tools it
should not.


!!!Building AI/MCP

!!CMake

The CMake options <*ENABLE_AI_MCP*> and <*ENABLE_AI_MCP_HTTP*> are off by default.
<*ENABLE_AI_MCP_HTTP*> turns on <*ENABLE_AI_MCP*>, and <*ENABLE_AI*> turns on both.

    cmake -B build -DENABLE_AI_MCP_HTTP=ON
    cmake --build build

!!GNU Make

The components <*AI/MCP*> and <*AI/MCP/HTTP*> are omitted from the typical configuration,
together with <*AI*>. Configure with <*--everything*>, or with an <*--omit*> list that does
not name <*AI*>.


!!!Examples

!!A Server

The following program serves one tool over the stdio transport. Its log output, if any, must
go to <*std::cerr*>: <*std::cout*> carries the protocol.

    #include "Poco/AI/MCP/Server.h"
    #include <iostream>

    using namespace Poco::AI::MCP;

    int main()
    {
        Server server;
        Implementation info;
        info.name = "greeter";
        info.version = "1.0";
        server.setServerInfo(info);
        server.registerTool("greet", "Greets a person by name.", nullptr,
            [](const Poco::JSON::Object::Ptr& pArgs)
            {
                ToolResult result;
                result.addText("Hello, " + pArgs->optValue<std::string>("name", "stranger") + "!");
                return result;
            });
        server.serve(std::cin, std::cout);
        return 0;
    }

!!A Client

The following program starts that server, lists its tools and calls one.

    #include "Poco/AI/MCP/ProcessClient.h"
    #include "Poco/JSON/Object.h"
    #include <iostream>

    using namespace Poco::AI::MCP;

    int main()
    {
        ProcessClient client("./greeter", {});
        const ServerDescription description = client.discover();
        std::cout << "Server: " << description.serverInfo.name << std::endl;
        for (const Tool& tool: client.listTools())
        {
            std::cout << tool.name << ": " << tool.description << std::endl;
        }
        Poco::JSON::Object::Ptr pArgs = new Poco::JSON::Object;
        pArgs->set("name", "World");
        const ToolResult result = client.callTool("greet", pArgs);
        std::cout << result.content()->getObject(0)->getValue<std::string>("text") << std::endl;
        return 0;
    }
